INT. CLOCKMAKER'S GUILD VAULT ANTECHAMBER - NIGHT

Marble floor. Brass pendulums under glass domes, swinging out of sync. A time lock gleams on a steel door.

A MAN in a janitor's coverall mops, eyes down. He is tall, mid-40s, with a limp. Behind him, a second MAN in a security blazer, younger, wide-shouldered, scrolls a tablet.

The janitor stops mopping and glances at the guard. The guard doesn't look up.

JANITOR
Quiet shift.

GUARD
Don't talk to me while I'm working.

JANITOR
Right.

He mops closer to the door. His mop handle taps the lock panel, twice, a soft tick.

GUARD
Hey. What was that?

JANITOR
Nothing. Sorry.

The guard approaches, tablet lowered. The janitor straightens. Their eyes meet.

GUARD
I don't think I know you.

JANITOR
Third week. Night crew.

GUARD
Name?

A beat. Somewhere, a pendulum clicks. The janitor's hand slips into his bucket.

JANITOR
Cormac Dwyer.

GUARD
(reading from tablet)
There's no Dwyer on the roster.

DWYER
(calmly)
No. There isn't.

He pulls a taser from the bucket and fires. The guard drops. Dwyer kneels, takes his keycard, and turns to the pendulums.

At the far end of the room, a SECOND MAN has stood watching from the shadows the entire time: a thin figure in a clockmaker's apron, silver hair, spectacles. He steps forward.

STRANGER
You're early.

DWYER
(not surprised)
You said midnight.

STRANGER
I said twelve-oh-three. He walks the floor at twelve-oh-two.

DWYER
Then he's dead on schedule.

The stranger smiles, tucks a loupe into his pocket, and removes a key from around his neck.

STRANGER
Call me Aurelio.

AURELIO inserts the key. The vault door sighs open on a room of ticking gold.
